      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:

** The current 4" chimney and 5" combustion air supply for the furnace.
** Some air leakage is coming through the frames of the old windows. There is also a signifcant frost build-up on the windows, refer to my comments in additional section of the report. I also suspect there is pink fibreglass insulation behind the frames of the old windows, pink fibreglass insulation is a good insulator but a poor air sealer. I would recommend adding spray foam insulation whenever you are replacing the older windows.
** The outside door - this door is not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping).
** Some of the electrical outlets in exterior walls of the house. Foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates.

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 130 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your main house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (15) rigid foam polystyrene insulation to the exterior walls. 

Savings: By adding 3 inches(R15) rigid foam extruded polystrene insulation to these walls, you could potentially save $ 490 in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is strongly recommended and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval recommendation. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
https://aea.nt.ca/program/energy-efficient-products/
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $4,940 is available for adding R-15 insulation to the 100% exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <foundation />
                <windows>
All the windows in this house (8 total) are double pane slider vinyl windows, that you may choose to upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STARÂ® certified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 90 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the Searchable product list of eligible windows for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

ENERGY STAR labels must be affixed to windows at the time of installation and remain in place until the time of your post-retrofit evaluation. If ENERGY STAR labels are removed prior to the post-retrofit evaluation, the following photos are your responsibility to acquire, retain and provide to the energy advisor at the time of the post-retrofit evaluation:
A photo of each newly-installed window showing a portion of the surrounding wall assembly from the inside, outside or both, AND

A photo of each ENERGY STAR label affixed to the window that clearly shows the ENERGY STAR certification mark, Manufacturer model code/number, U-factor/Energy Rating, NRCan reference number or a Certified Products Directory (CPD) number, and a Certified mark from one of the following:
? Canadian Standards Association (CSA)
? Intertek Canada
? Keystone Certifications
? Labtest Certification (LC)
? QAI Laboratories
? National Fenestration Council (NFRC)

Removed labels must be submitted and provided to the energy advisor.

Request your supplier/installer to include the following information on the invoice:
? Name of the homeowner and address of the house;
? Date of installation;
? Window make and model numbers;
? NRCan Reference Number;
? Indication of whether windows are ENERGY STAR or ENERGY STAR Most Efficient certified;
? Number of windows and location of each installation;
? U-factor/Energy Rating (ER);
? Itemized cost of each window and total cost of installation; and
? Itemization of any other work done as part of the installation, such as air/gap sealing and associated costs.

Please note that a window schedule (quote) cannot be used as a substitute for the aforementioned acceptable window validations and cannot be submitted to the portal as a standalone document to validate window installation and eligibility. However, the window schedule may be attached to the invoice provided there is a reference on the invoice to a schedule reference number to link the two documents.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>
During my assessment, I noticed excess moisture and frost build up around most of the windows. there appeared to beminimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.

I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.

Savings: By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, you could potentially increase your annual energy usage by 2 GJ and it will cost you $100 in energy cost. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Here are some of the easier ways to tackle excess moisture in your house:

Â Open all blinds, curtains, and shutters to ensure proper airflow over your windows.
Â Move furniture or other things away from the floor registers to allow airflow.
Â Run the range hood fan in the kitchen when cooking. Your current range hood fan is a recirculator which means it is recirculating the air inside the house as opposed to venting outside, you may want to consider connecting the range hood fan to the existing outside vent.
Â Running the bathroom exhaust fan for at least 30 minutes after a shower or a bath and if possible, install a humidistat which will help maintain humidity levels in a preferred range, working in tandem with the bathroom fan. When the humidity increases, the humidistat works to lessen the humidity by removing it from air. It shuts off when the humidity has dropped below a certain level, which is 40-50% for the normal bathroom.

If youÂre still having challenges lowering humidity levels, you may want to consider buying a dehumidifier.

Moisture in the home is also very dependant on the occupants of the home and living styles. Contributors to additional moisture in a home can be more people breathing in a home, plants, cooking, showering, cleaning, hanging clothes to dry, etc. Depending on lifestyle, even with an HRV/ERV you can experience some moisture issues. 

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>
You currently have a 151 litre gas-fired hot water tank. You may want to consider replacing your gas-fired hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.5 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with a gas-fired furnace with an efficiency of 80%. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat and installing a dedicated combustion air inlet for the pellet stove.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower and installing a dedicated combustion air inlet for your pellet stove, you could potentially save $ 940 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28 GJ, 

By installing 96% AFUE condensing furnace with an ECM fan blower, you could qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

Note: When you install a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace you no longer required the 5" combustion air pipe. I would recommend converting that into a fresh air supply pipe connecting directly to the return ductwork of the furnace and include a manual damper so that you can adjust between fully open, fully closed or part way in between - depending on the indoor air quality. If the air inside is stale &amp; higher humidity, open the damper up more. If the air feels dry &amp; lots of static electricity, you can close the damper up more. Each time you operate an exhaust fan (bathroom, dryer, range hood), outside fresh air will be drawn in &amp; distributed through the furnace ductwork to each room - helping to reduce window frost.  
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


Pellet Stove:

I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency furnace and the cost of pellets in Fort Simpson at the $6.57/bag rate) if you use your current pellet stove, used for about 50% of the heating requirement for the main floor of your house. Getting that pellet stove heat throughout the house is something your heating contractor can work on by using the new furnace fan to circulate the pellet heat throughout the house. I modeled an upgrade that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe.

Savings: By using your current 70.3% efficient pellet stove for 50% of the heating requirements of your house, you could potentially save $ 190 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 97% AFUE propane furnace with the ECM blower motor. </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>
During my assessment, I noticed excess moisture and frost build up around most of the windows. there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home.

Moisture in the home is dependant on the occupants of the home and living styles. Contributors to additional moisture in a home can be more people breathing in a home, plants, cooking, showering, cleaning, hanging clothes to dry, etc

Fresh air pipe: When you install a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace you no longer required the 5" combustion air pipe. I would recommend converting that into a fresh air supply pipe connecting directly to the return ductwork of the furnace and include a manual damper so that you can adjust between fully open, fully closed or part way in between - depending on the indoor air quality. If the air inside is stale &amp; higher humidity, open the damper up more. If the air feels dry &amp; lots of static electricity, you can close the damper up more. Each time you operate an exhaust fan (bathroom, dryer, range hood), outside fresh air will be drawn in &amp; distributed through the furnace ductwork to each room - helping to reduce window frost. 

Here are some of the easier steps to tackle excess moisture in your house:

Â Open all blinds, curtains, and shutters to ensure proper airflow over your windows.
Â Move furniture or other things away from the floor registers to allow airflow.
Â Run the range hood fan in the kitchen when cooking. Your current range hood fan is a recirculator which means it is recirculating the air inside the house as opposed to venting outside, you may want to consider connecting the range hood fan to the existing outside vent.
Â Running the bathroom exhaust fan for at least 30 minutes after a shower or a bath and if possible, install a humidistat which will help maintain humidity levels in a preferred range, working in tandem with the bathroom fan. When the humidity increases, the humidistat works to lessen the humidity by removing it from air. It shuts off when the humidity has dropped below a certain level, which is 40-50% for the normal bathroom.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:
- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.
Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights Â Non-hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort Simpson, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.5 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of these equipments are not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current propane furnace, domestic hot water tank and pellet stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. 

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 
        - a bathroom fan
        - a clothes dryer

I did see a vent for the range hood fan outside and I suspect it is not connected right now. If it is indeed not connected, I suggest hooking up the range hood fan to the outside vent as opposed to recirculating inside your house. You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs Â Fort Simpson

The following Dec. 1, 2022 values, excluding GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to subsidy limit, after subsidy $0.69 per kWh
Oil - $1.23 per liter 
Propane - $0.79 per liter delivered
Wood - $400 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $6.57 per bag, $328 per tonne bulk delivered

** Oil &amp; Biomass fuels in Fort Simpson tend to experience cyclical fuel price volatility. The Arctic Energy Alliance therefore uses 5-year rolling average for these values to better help you evaluate longer term heating appliance purchase/upgrade decisions.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Abdul Mohammed, Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
